y wondering why i summoned you  just as the ocean accepts the rivers of  all the world  so this place accepts all the souls  if even one person commits a sin here  every last one of us will die welcome to  life under the golden rule  it's a ghastly thing is it not  the many shall suffer  for the sins of the one  forgotten city for me was  easily one of the most profound and  revelatory experiences that i've had in  gaming not only this year but i think in  quite a long while the whole concept of  this game is you falling into the city  where you learn that  basically there's only one specific rule  if anyone commits a sin any single  person  everyone gets killed and turned to gold  because you're from the future  you know that that is going to happen  and it's up to you to figure out  why everyone's going to turn to gold and  so that starts this really fascinating  mystery that just sets you off to just  go and explore and get to know these  brilliantly thought out and interesting  characters that all feel really fleshed  out all feel very real and yet at the  same time this game takes that like very  personified story and basically just  turns it into a philosophical argument  because the entire idea of this game is  to make you ask questions of what is a  sin who gets to define sins and it  starts to get you to ask questions that  philosophy and philosophers have been  asking for  for ages and it's really just delving  into that and making it philosophy feel  pertinent and immediate in a way that i  think few other games and stories really  ever have done and because it's a game  it allows you to invest in it and place  yourself into it and have your yourself  asked those questions in such unique  ways but also because it is a time loop  you're also allowed to within the  narrative of the game play around to  test the boundaries of these rules on  top of that the game's atmosphere and  way that it evokes different themes and  ideas are  so incredibly intelligent t